From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: foxnne <foxnne@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:29:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Wayland: frame insets, for a shadow the application draws
 round its own decorations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An application that draws its own decorations on Wayland draws its drop shadow too, in a
transparent margin of its surface, as GTK, libadwaita, Chromium, Firefox and libdecor do. The
compositor has to be told which part of the surface is the window — xdg_surface.set_window_geometry
— or it places, snaps and tiles the shadow with it, and the margin has to pass clicks through.
SDL sets the window geometry to the whole surface (and only without a viewport), so an SDL
application could not.

Four creation properties, SDL_PROP_WINDOW_CREATE_WAYLAND_FRAME_INSET_{LEFT,TOP,RIGHT,BOTTOM}_NUMBER,
give the margins in window coordinates. While an xdg-shell toplevel floats:

- the size the compositor configures is the frame's, and the surface grows by the insets round it;
- the window geometry is the frame, with or without a viewport;
- the input region is the frame and a band round it, where a hit test can answer the edges it
  resizes from: SDL_PROP_WINDOW_CREATE_WAYLAND_FRAME_INPUT_MARGIN_NUMBER units (default 8, 0 for
  the frame alone), at most each side's inset;
- an opaque window's opaque region is the frame, not the shadow round it;
- the min/max sizes sent are the frame's, and the toplevel bounds a compositor sends (which bound
  the frame) are widened by the insets before SDL clamps a window to them;
- popups are anchored to the parent's frame.

Maximized, tiled or fullscreen the insets are zero. The insets in effect are published as
SDL_PROP_WINDOW_WAYLAND_FRAME_INSET_*_NUMBER from creation on (those asked for, zero for libdecor),
and change with the window's state. libdecor windows are untouched: libdecor frames its own (an
application drawing its own decorations sets SDL_HINT_VIDEO_WAYLAND_ALLOW_LIBDECOR to 0).

The insets asked for are also kept in the window's own properties (SDL.internal.wayland.
frame_inset.*), so a window SDL makes again (SDL_RecreateWindow, for an OpenGL renderer or a
reconnect) keeps them. Hiding a window destroys its xdg_surface and the window geometry with it,
so hiding forgets the insets applied, and the configure that shows it again frames it again.

Fizzy draws its Linux window's title bar and shadow with these (fizzy's linux_titlebar.zig).

Upstream main (not release-3.4.x) gained its own version on 2026-09-24 (5d7167cb, with 86b89cff
and dff05080): opted into with SDL_PROP_WINDOW_CREATE_WAYLAND_ENABLE_INSETS_BOOLEAN, the insets
set as SDL_PROP_WINDOW_WAYLAND_BORDER_INSET_*_NUMBER and read at each configure, with no input
band and the same opaque-region, bounds and recreate problems. At the update onto a release that
has it, fizzy moves to upstream's API and keeps only what upstream lacks here.
